      "writtenOn": "2024-03-28T05:59:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In the offline discussion, an idea came out; java API `getting supported OS list` doesn\u0027t spawn and communicate with virtmgr via AIDL, just getting the list by itself. And I think this is pretty good one, to solve it. Would anyone have other opinions not to do this?",
      "revId": "d658c51732e3691f0a5c5b29562044cf40751dff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d44c745f_2dd6a761",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1950816
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-29T02:44:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Seems the amount of the task is getting bigger. Initially I thought it\u0027s just seeking file system though, `getSupportedOsList` needs to check if the feature `vendor_module` is enabled, and it requires to communicate with `virtmgr`.\n\nAlternative option is letting Java API can know whether a feature is enabled by itself, not communicating with `virtmgr`.\n\nHowever then, why should other APIs in `VirtualMachineManager` like `isFeatureEnabled` require `MANAGE_VIRTUAL_MACHINE`, but `getSupportedOsList ` doesn\u0027t? IIUC, I think those APIs should have same permission level, couldn\u0027t we change it? @inseob@google.com  @jiyong@google.com",
      "parentUuid": "03c12e65_8dea2b1e",
      "revId": "d658c51732e3691f0a5c5b29562044cf40751dff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "e71a8237_139342ff",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1132673
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-03-29T08:29:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The point is that `getSupportedOsList` doesn\u0027t need to be implemented inside of virtmgr. \n\n\u003e  why should other APIs in VirtualMachineManager like isFeatureEnabled require MANAGE_VIRTUAL_MACHINE\n\nI think APIs like `isSomethingEnabled` don\u0027t have to enforce the permission. Simply querying the capability doesn\u0027t have to be protected, IMO. @alanstokes@google.com do you agree?",
      "parentUuid": "d44c745f_2dd6a761",
      "revId": "d658c51732e3691f0a5c5b29562044cf40751dff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3b2589b5_85120931",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1060831
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-04-02T15:14:03Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e I think APIs like isSomethingEnabled don\u0027t have to enforce the permission. Simply querying the capability doesn\u0027t have to be protected, IMO. \n\nWell, there is some precedent: VirtualMachineManager#getCapabilities doesn\u0027t require any permission.\n\nHowever, isRemoteAttestationSupported() and isUpdatableVmSupported() do require the permission, and that\u0027s part of the API definition, and we\u0027re no longer allowed to change it.\n\nOTOH getSupportedOSList() is not documented to require the permission, and again we can\u0027t change that. So it must not. So implementing it in the client code is fine, if we can make that work.\n\nBut I also think we need to change things so trying to start a VM when you don\u0027t have the permission (and hence the gid) fails more cleanly. That could probably be a separate bug.",
      "parentUuid": "e71a8237_139342ff",
      "revId": "d658c51732e3691f0a5c5b29562044cf40751dff",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
